{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## THIRD CONTRADICTED ENTRY — and this one is a SAME-DIGEST contradiction, which sharpens the argument\n\nRetracted on `docs/jira-dc-capability-map.md` in Gerrit **1394** (patchset 2).\n\n**The claim:** [req-0022] — \"two distinct issue-type ids (10003 and 10004) are both named `Task`,\nso name-based type resolution is ambiguous\".\n\n**The measurement:** harness run **30951453979** (`main` @ `d394a70529`), `GET\n/rest/api/2/issuetype` → HTTP 200, `[2e47-issue-type-evidence]`:\n\n    instance-wide entries matching ['Task','Sub-task','Epic']:\n      [('10002', 'Task'), ('10003', 'Sub-task'), ('10000', 'Epic')]\n\nExactly **one** id named `Task`, and **10003 is `Sub-task`, not `Task`**. The emitter\n(`tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py`) filters the full `/issuetype` response on those three\nnames with **no de-duplication**, so a second `Task` would have printed had one existed.\n\n## Why this is the strongest evidence yet for a deterministic re-measurement pass\n\nThe running tally of map entries contradicted by live measurement is now **three**:\n\n| entry | claim | measured | retracted in |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| req-0071/0072/0073 | DC rejects a 255-char label (ceiling 254) | 254 **and** 255 accepted, read back | 1389 |\n| req-0022 | ids 10003 + 10004 both named `Task` | one `Task` (10002); 10003 = `Sub-task` | 1394 ps2 |\n| — | description/comment ceiling | never observed at all; only read as *configured* | open (AC3 here) |\n\n**The load-bearing detail: the digest was IDENTICAL.** The `FROM` pin at `d394a70529` is\nbyte-identical to the one the map records, so run 30951453979 measured the same pinned base image\nthe answers describe. This is therefore **not** base-image drift — it is precisely the failure\nmode this ticket exists for: **a wrong answer at an unchanged digest**, which the new digest gate\nprovably cannot catch.\n\nThat is worth being exact about, because it upgrades the argument from \"the map could go stale\" to\n\"the map has been wrong twice on the image we actually run, and neither instance was detectable by\nany check we had\". The digest gate landed in 259b is still what makes this *diagnosable* — with the\npin asserted, \"different image\" is ruled out mechanically rather than argued — but ruling out one\nexplanation is not the same as detecting the defect.\n\n**What remains undetermined**, and deliberately not overstated in the doc: issue-type ids are\nassigned as instance state is built, and the mapping run created a project from **each** candidate\ntemplate while the harness provisions a **single** Scrum project. So the map's reading may reflect\ninstance state its own broader provisioning produced rather than a misreading of its evidence.\nEither way the *conclusion* does not hold for the environment the harness runs.\n\n**This tightens AC1 here:** a deterministic pass must re-assert claims **against the provisioning\nstate it declares**, not merely against the image digest. Two runs of the same image with\ndifferent provisioning are not comparable, and an id-valued claim is not portable between them —\nwhich is an argument for the contract pinning issue types by NAME-to-ID resolution performed at\nsetup, rather than recording raw ids as facts about \"the image\"."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785884632495086001,"uuid":"f47726fa-1583-40d6-8bd3-a58e12f9f33c"}